{
	"name": "Topological Sort",
	"summary": "Orders the nodes of a directed graph so that every node comes after all the nodes it depends on.",
	"description": [
		"Topological Sort puts the nodes of a directed graph in a line so that every edge points forward: if there is an edge from A to B, A comes before B. Think of tasks with dependencies, such as courses and their prerequisites or the build steps of a project. It only works on a directed acyclic graph (DAG), because the nodes of a cycle all wait for each other and none of them could ever come first.",
		"This version is Kahn's algorithm. It first counts the in-degree of every node, the number of edges that point to it. Nodes with an in-degree of 0 depend on nothing, so they go into a queue. Then it repeatedly takes a node from the queue, appends it to the order and removes its outgoing edges. Whenever the in-degree of a node drops to 0, everything it depends on is already in the order, so it joins the queue.",
		"Every node and every edge is handled once, so Kahn's algorithm runs in O(V + E) time, where V is the number of nodes and E the number of edges. A graph often has many valid orders, and the one you get depends on the order of the queue. If the order ends up shorter than the number of nodes, the remaining nodes form a cycle, so Kahn's algorithm also detects cycles."
	],
	"complexity": {
